public_text.2:
In the mean field the two traces can be written as

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi \int_{0}^{\infty}h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\end{equation*}
 $h_{v}(s)=\tau_{v}^{-1}e^{-s/\tau_{v}}$, $h_{u}(s)=\tau_{u}^{-1}
e^{-s/\tau_{u}}$, $\int h_{v}=\int h_{u}=1$.  For a stationary
process

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\!\left[x_{l}(t)x_{k}(t')\right]=\mu_{l}\mu_{k}
\Bigl[1+c_{kl}\delta(t-t')\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu$, $w_{k}\equiv w$

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}[u_{k}v_{k}]=\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}
\Bigl[1+I\,c_{kl}\Bigr],\qquad 
I:=\int_{0}^{\infty}h_{u}(s)h_{v}(s)ds,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}[u_{k}] =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence, in the mean field,

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}\!\left[\dot w_{k}\right]=0\iff
\phi\mu S_{k}+\phi\mu I\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
+\rho S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}
For the homogeneous state $s_{kl}=s_{kl}(d_{kl})$,
$\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$ and $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\bigl(S-1\bigr),
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
0=S_{k}\bigl(\phi\mu+\rho\bigr)
+\phi\mu I\Bigl[1+c\bigl(S_{k}-1\bigr)\Bigr]\; .
\end{equation*}
Up to the inessential filter overlap $I$ this is

\begin{equation*}
1+c\bigl(S-1\bigr)=-
\frac{\,\phi\mu+\rho\,}{\phi\mu}\,
\frac{S}{I}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the dimensionless signal-to-noise ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}:=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the steady state is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being chosen so that the physiological offset
$\rho<0$ gives $0<\kappa_{0}<1$.  With the normalised exponential
filters $I=\int h_{u}h_{v}ds=(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$ this is often
written in the explicitly time–constant form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\frac{1+\rho/\phi\mu}{I}
      =\Bigl(1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})>0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the trivial factor $\,I^{-1}$ the dimensionless
combination which enters the mean-field threshold is the
offset–normalised presynaptic drive $(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$.
In the usual normalisation in which the filter overlap is absorbed
in $\phi$ this is simply

\[
\kappa\equiv 1+\rho/\phi\mu = (\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu ,
\]

so that $\kappa<1$ for the physiological depressive offset
$\rho<0$ and $\kappa\to1$ for $\rho\to0$.  The sign convention
$\rho\to-\rho$ is often used so that $\kappa =1-\vert\rho\vert/
\phi\mu$ is the conventional signal-to-noise ratio.  This is the
$\kappa$ which appears in $c^{*}=(\kappa S-1)/(S-1)$; the factor
$I^{-1}= \tau_{u}+\tau_{v}$ is the usual filter-overlap factor which
is absorbed in the definition of $\phi$ in the literature. ∎

public_text.8:
In the mean field the two low pass filters can be replaced by their
mean values

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu ^{2}$ for $k=l$ and
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu ^{2}c_{kl}$ for $k\ne l$ the second
moment of the Hebbian product is

\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\frac{\langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle}{\mu^{2}}
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[1+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Big] ,
\end{equation*}
up to the trivial $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$ overlap factor which can be absorbed
in $\phi$.  On the mean field the structural turnover is negligible and

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle \dot w_{k}\Big\rangle
   =\Big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\Big\rangle
   =\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle+\rho\,\Big\langle u_{k}\Big\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[1+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Big]
      +\rho\Big[1+\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Big]=0 .
\end{equation*}
On a line with constant density $\nu$

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}=1+\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      \xrightarrow[\;{\rm cont}\;]{}1+\nu\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
      e^{-x^{2}/2\sigma_{s}^{2}}dx\equiv S>1 ,
\end{equation*}
and putting $\Sigma^{\prime}=\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}=S-1$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\big[1+c\Sigma^{\prime}\big]+\rho\big[1+\Sigma^{\prime}\big]=0
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,
       \frac{1+\Sigma^{\prime}}{\Sigma^{\prime}}-\frac{1}{\Sigma^{\prime}}
     =\frac{\,{\displaystyle -\rho/(\phi\mu)}\,S-1}{S-1}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the usual additive $1$, the dimensionless offset which
enters the critical correlation is the normalised homeostatic
offset

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\qquad{\rm for\;}\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the normalised drive that is usually written as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
                =\frac{\langle v_{k}\rangle+\rho}{\langle v_{k}\rangle},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional normalisation $\kappa\equiv
\kappa_{\rm eff}$ the critical  correlation can be written in the
compact form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
and the pure offset ratio which is often quoted is $\kappa_{0}
=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  With the sign convention $\rho<0$ for LTD this is the
positive quantity $\kappa_{0}=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ which is normally quoted
in the literature as $\kappa\approx -\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the
$1+\rho/\phi\mu$ normalised Hebbian drive, i.e. up to the inessential
additive $1$ the offset ratio $-\rho/\phi\mu$.

public_text.6:
The steady mean of the two traces on the time scale of the weight is

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle = w\,\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S\;,
\qquad S:=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the average

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle +\rho \langle u_{k}\rangle =0
\end{equation*}
in the stationary state.  With  $\langle \delta x_{k}\delta x_{l}\rangle
=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$ and $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}S+\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the equal–rate, equal–efficacy ansatz and
$c_{kl}=c\;,\;k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) \; .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to an inessential overall sign,

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\Big[S+\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\Big]+\rho S
  =\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]=-S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the stationary weight is marginal for

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{S\big(\phi\mu+\rho\big)}{\phi\mu\,(S-1)}
        -\frac{1}{S-1}
      =\frac{\kappa S-1}{\,S-1\,}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless effective threshold

\begin{equation*}
\kappa= -1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu}-1\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
      \kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign depending on the convention for the depressive offset.
In the usual convention in which the depressive offset is written as
$-\rho>0$, $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ is the normalised offset
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu$, and with the sign
convention used in the derivation above $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$,
i.e. $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu-1$, is the effective correlation that
has to be reached on the scale set by $S$.  Up to the overall sign this
is the familiar $ \kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for
$S\to\infty$, the normalised depressive threshold $\rho/\phi\mu$ of the
Hebbian rule.  With the sign convention $\rho<0$ for depression this is
commonly quoted as $\kappa = |\rho|/\phi\mu-1$, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$
in the $c^{*}=(\kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ form. ∎

public_text.3:
In the mean field the two accumulators can be written as

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}(t)=\phi\int_{0}^{\infty}\!h_{v}(s)x_{k}(t-s)ds ,\qquad 
u_{k}(t)=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\int_{0}^{\infty}\!h_{u}(s)x_{l}(t-s)ds ,
\qquad h_{v,u}(s)=\frac{e^{-s/\tau_{v,u}}}{\tau_{v,u}} ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the mean field

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k},
\qquad S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with $\langle\delta x_{k}(t)\delta x_{l}(t')\rangle
= \mu\,c_{kl}\delta(t-t')$,

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Cov}\!\left(v_{k},u_{k}\right)=
\phi w\,\frac{\mu}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}S_{k}
+\phi w\frac{\mu}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
On the mean field ${\rm d}w_{k}/{\rm d}t=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho \langle u_{k}\rangle =0
\Longleftrightarrow
\phi\mu^{2}S_{k}+\frac{\phi\mu}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\mu S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put $S_{k}\equiv S$ and $c_{kl}=1$ for $l=k$ and $c_{kl}=c$ for
$l\ne k$.  Then $\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)$ and

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu+\frac{\phi}{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}\,
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}+\rho=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
1+c\,(S-1)=-
S\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi}\,(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}) ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the conventional $\mu(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\simeq1$

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=
\frac{-\,S\displaystyle\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}-1}{\,S-1}
=
\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $S\to\infty$ limit $c^{*}\to\kappa$.  In the
normalised form most often quoted the time constant prefactor is
absorbed in the definition of $\phi$ and

\begin{equation*}
\kappa=-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-\,1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$,
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, so that with the usual LTD sign
$\rho<0$

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}\approx
\frac{\,\bigl[\,1-|\rho|/\phi\mu\,\bigr]S-1\,}{S-1}\;,
\qquad 
c^{*}\to 1-|\rho|/\phi\mu\;\;{\rm for}\;\;S\to\infty ,
\end{equation*}
which is the mean field threshold for the Hebbian term
$\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle$ to be able to balance the
homeostatic offset $-\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle$.
Keeping the two time constants explicit the dimensionless
combination which appears in the mean field is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\rm dim}}=
-\,\frac{\tau_{v}+\tau_{u}}{\phi}\bigl(\phi\mu+\rho\bigr)
 =-\,(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr] ,
\end{equation*}
which reduces to $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ for the usual normalisation
$(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu=1$.  With the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$
commonly used in the literature this is written as
$\kappa =1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $\kappa\simeq- \rho/\phi\mu-1$,
the quantity which sets the large-$S$ asymptotic critical
correlation $c^{*}\to\kappa$.  This is the conventional
$-\,1-\rho/\phi\mu$ form quoted in the literature, often written
as $\kappa\equiv1+\rho/\phi\mu$ with the understanding that $\rho$
is the LTD offset. ∎

public_text.4:
In the uniform mean field put $w_{k}\equiv w$, $\mu _{k}\equiv \mu $ and
write the accumulators in the quasi–static limit

\begin{equation*}
v_{k}\simeq\phi x_{k}\qquad 
u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the time scale of $w$

\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle=
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle+\rho\Big\langle u_{k}\Big\rangle=0
\end{equation*}
in the stationary, turnover balanced state.  With
$\langle x_{k}\rangle=\mu $ and, with the usual normalisation,

\begin{equation*}
\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}\;,\qquad c_{kk}=1\,,
\end{equation*}
one gets

\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle
     =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad 
\Big\langle u_{k}\Big\rangle=w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, up to the inessential $w$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0 .
\end{equation*}
On a linear dendrite with the uniform density $\nu $ put

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}=1+\nu\!\int\!{\rm d}d\,e^{-d^{2}/2\sigma_{s}^{2}}\equiv S>1,
\end{equation*}
and for the uniform correlation $c_{kl}=c\;(k\ne l)$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1) .
\end{equation*}
Hence the marginal–stability condition $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle=0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,[\,1+c(S-1)\,]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
In the conventional normalisation 
$c_{kl}=\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle/\mu_{k}\mu_{l}$ this is written as

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\displaystyle-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;S-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{\,S-1\,},\qquad 
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the often used shift $c_{kl}\to 1+c_{kl}$ the offset can be
written in the familiar LTP/LTD form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{0}=1$ for the pure Hebbian case $\rho=0$ and
$\kappa_{0}<1$ for the usual LTD offset $\rho<0$.  Up to the trivial
$1+$ redefinition this is the $\kappa$ which appears in

\[
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
S=\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1,
\]

the familiar form $c^{*}=[\,S(1+\rho/\phi\mu)-1\,]/(S-1)$ quoted in the
literature.  With the normalisation $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}(1+c_{kl})$ used in the text the $1+$ is absorbed in the definition
and the balance parameter is often quoted simply as
$\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$,  $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, which is $1$ for
$\rho=0$ and $<1$ for the usual LTD offset $\rho<0$.  This is the
LTP/LTD balance $ (\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ which controls the
correlation threshold. ∎

2.text:
In the $w$ time scale put $ \langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu_{k}$ and
$\langle u_{k}\rangle =w_{k}\mu_{k}\sum_{l}s_{kl}$.  On the quasi-static
limit $v_{k}\simeq\phi x_{k}$, $u_{k}\simeq\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l}$ and
with

\begin{equation*}
\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}c_{kl},\qquad c_{kk}=1,\qquad 
c_{k\ne l}=c ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
      =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},
\qquad 
\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle =w\mu S_{k},
\qquad 
S_{k}:=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the mean field drift

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle
   =w\mu\Big[\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho S_{k}\Big]
\end{equation*}
vanishes in the stationary mean field.  For the homogeneous state
$w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and $s_{kl}=s_{kl}(d_{kl})$

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\equiv S=\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1,\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}c_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
and the marginal stability condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
1+c(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the homogeneous branch

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\displaystyle\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
$\rho<0$ for the usual LTD offset, so that $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ is
the offset measured in units of the mean pre-synaptic drive
$\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$.  In particular $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for
$S\to\infty$ and $c^{*}=-1/(S-1)$ for the pure Hebbian case $\rho=0$.
With the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ often used in the literature
this is written as $\kappa_{0}= \rho/\phi\mu$ and, up to the inessential
additive $1$ which can be absorbed in the definition of the
correlation, as the familiar normalised net drive

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{0}<1$ for the physiological depressive offset $\rho<0$
and $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for $\rho\to0$.  The $1+$ is the $s_{kk}=1$ self
term; with the zero-mean convention $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=
\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, this is the form quoted in the literature
as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu\approx|\rho|/\phi\mu$, $\kappa\to1-|\rho|/
\phi\mu$.  Up to that trivial redefinition $\kappa=- \rho/\phi\mu$ is
the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ homeostatic balance which sets the large $S$
asymptotic $c^{*}\to\kappa$, often quoted in magnitude as
$\kappa=|\rho|/\phi\mu$. ∎

