1.text:
In the mean field the two traces can be replaced on the time scale of
$w$ by their stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu_{k},\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w_{l}\mu_{l}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k},
\qquad S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl} .
\end{equation*}
With the homogeneous ansatz $w_{k}=w,\;\mu_{k}=\mu$ and
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1),\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle
   =\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
   =w\mu\Big[\,\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho S\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the inessential overall factor $w\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the marginal stability line

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal condition in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic ratio which controls the threshold is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\,\mu}>0\qquad{\rm for\;the\;physiological\;
LTD\;offset\;}\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
so that $|\kappa|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$ is the homeostatic offset measured in
units of the mean pre-synaptic drive $\phi\mu$.  In particular
$c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$ and for an isolated synapse
$S\to1$, $c^{*}\to\kappa=1$, i.e. $-\rho=\phi\mu$, the usual
LTP/LTD balance.

With the correlation normalised as $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle
=\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the self term $s_{kk}=1$
is absorbed in $S$ and the same threshold is conventionally written as

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $\kappa_{0}<1$ for the physiological depressive offset
$\rho<0$ and $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for $\rho\to0$.  Up to the trivial
$1+$ redefinition $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$ is the
normalised net drive $(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ which is normally quoted
in the literature as $\kappa\simeq|\rho|/\phi\mu$.  With the sign
convention $\rho\to-\rho$ this is the familiar positive ratio
$\kappa=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, often written simply as $\kappa=\rho/\phi\mu$.
The filter overlap $I=\int h_{u}h_{v}ds=(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$ can be
kept explicit, $\kappa_{\rm dim}= -\big[1+\rho/\phi\mu\big]/I$, i.e.
$\kappa_{\rm dim}\propto(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})$, and is absorbed in the
definition of $\phi$ in the normalisation used above. ∎

2.text:
In the $w$--time scale the two filters can be taken in the
quasi-static limit

$$v_{k}\simeq \phi x_{k},\qquad 
u_{k}\simeq \sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}x_{l} $$
so that with $\langle x_{k}\rangle =\mu_{k}$, 
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}$ and $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w_{k}\mu_{k}S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}:=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w_{k}\mu_{k}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the mean field

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
   =w_{k}\mu_{k}\Big[\phi\mu_{k}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
          +\rho S_{k}\Big] .
\end{equation*}
For the homogeneous state $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$, 
$c_{kl}=c$ for $k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\equiv S=\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1 ,\qquad
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
    =1+c\,(S-1) ,
\end{equation*}
so that the marginal mean field condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c^{*}(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
Writing the balance in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic ratio which enters the threshold is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa =-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude $\kappa =|\rho|/\phi\mu$,
$\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$.  With the conventional LTD sign
$\rho<0$ this is the positive ratio $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ of the additive
homeostatic offset to the mean pre-synaptic drive.  In particular
$c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$ and $c^{*}=-1/(S-1)$ for the pure
Hebbian case $\rho=0$.

Up to the usual $1+$ redefinition of the correlation,
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\big[1+c_{kl}\big]$, $c_{kk}=0$,
this is normally quoted as the normalised net drive

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that with the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ the familiar form
$c^{*}=(\kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ is obtained with
$\kappa_{0}<1$ for the physiological depressive offset $\rho<0$ and
$\kappa_{0}\to1$ for $\rho\to0$.  The $1+$ is the $s_{kk}=1$ self-term;
absorbed in $S$ this is the often quoted $\kappa_{0}\simeq
|\rho|/\phi\mu$.  This is the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ homeostatic balance
which sets the large-$S$ asymptotic $c^{*}\to\kappa$. ∎

8.text:
In the quasi–static mean field
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle=w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with the usual rate normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$,
$c_{k\ne l}=c$,
\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}
For the homogeneous state $w_{k}=w$, $\mu_{k}=\mu$,
\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
 =1+c\big(S-1\big),\qquad S\equiv S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1,
\end{equation*}
so that on the $w$ time scale
\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\propto
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
=w\mu\Big\{\phi\mu\big[\,1+c(S-1)\,\big]+\rho S\Big\}.
\end{equation*}
Hence the marginal mean–field balance is
\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\big[\,1+c^{*}(S-1)\,\big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S\;,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{-\rho/(\phi\mu)\,S-1}{S-1}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting
\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\qquad \rho<0
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic ratio of the additive LTD offset
$|\rho|$ to the mean pre–synaptic drive $\phi\mu$, the threshold can be
written in the familiar percolation form
\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
c^{*}\xrightarrow[S\to\infty]{}\kappa ,
\end{equation*}
which is the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ balance that is usually quoted.  With the
conventional shift $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$,
$c_{kk}=0$, the self term $1$ is absorbed in the definition of the
correlation and the same threshold is written as
\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
so that $\kappa_{0}<1$ for the physiological depressive offset $\rho<0$
and $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for $\rho\to0$.  Up to the trivial $1+$ redefinition
and the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ this is $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu
\approx -\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. the normalised net mean drive
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, and with the filter overlap $I$ absorbed in
$\phi$ the explicit time-constant form is $\kappa_{\rm dim}\propto
(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi$, which reduces to the
dimensionless ratio above for the usual normalisation
$(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})\mu=1$.  In the sign convention in which the
depressive offset is taken positive this is the familiar
$\kappa=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.

5.text:
In the stationary mean field put $w_{k}=w$, $\mu _{k}=\mu$ and
$ {\rm E}[x_{k}x_{l}]=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$ with $c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$,
$k\ne l$.  On the $w$–time scale

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}:=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv S ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[\,s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}\Big]
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big] .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the mean field

\begin{equation*}
0={\rm E}\big[\,\dot w_{k}\,\big]\propto 
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
 =w\mu\Big\{\phi\mu\big[\,1+c(S-1)\big]+\rho S\Big\},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the inessential overall $w$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\big[\,1+c(S-1)\big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
so that on the marginal branch

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\,S .
\end{equation*}
With the self term $s_{kk}=1$ kept explicit this can be written in the
percolation form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\rho/(\phi\mu)\,S-1}{S-1}
     =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\qquad 
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0,
\end{equation*}
so that in magnitude $\kappa=|\rho|/(\phi\mu)$ is the homeostatic
offset measured in units of the mean pre-synaptic drive
$\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$.  For the physiological LTD offset
$\rho<0$ the quantity $-\rho/(\phi\mu)$ is $>0$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa$
for $S\to\infty$.  With the conventional shift $c_{kl}\to1+c_{kl}$,
$c_{kk}=0$, the self contribution $1$ is absorbed in $S$ and the
control parameter is quoted in the $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{0}<1$ for $\rho<0$ and $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for $\rho\to0$.
Up to the inessential $1+$ redefinition and the sign convention
$\rho\to-\rho$ this is the normalised net drive
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, often written simply as 
$\kappa\approx |\rho|/(\phi\mu)$ with the sign convention
$\kappa=- \rho/(\phi\mu$).  The $1+$ form is the
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ which is normally quoted as
$c^{*}= \big(\kappa_{0}S-1\big)/(S-1)$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
large spatial summation.  In the bare $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation this is
$\kappa=-\rho/(\phi\mu)$, i.e. $|\kappa|=|\rho|/(\phi\mu)$, the
LTP/LTD balance which sets the large-$S$ asymptotic critical
correlation. ∎

3.text:
In the uniform mean field put $w_{k}\equiv w,\;\mu _{k}\equiv \mu $ and
with the fast filter normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\tau_{w}
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho \big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad 
\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle =w\mu S_{k}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1),\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the inessential $w$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the marginal branch

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S \;,
\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{-\dfrac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S-1}{S-1}\; .
\end{equation*}
Writing

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic ratio which controls the threshold is
the offset normalised by the mean pre-synaptic drive

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\qquad{\rm for\;}\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
$|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the usual LTD balance $|\rho|=\phi\mu$
for an isolated synapse, $S\to1$.  With the self term $s_{kk}=1$ kept
explicit this is often written in the net-drive form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm net}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the convention $c_{kl}\to 1+c_{kl}$ used in the text
$1+c(S-1)=\kappa_{\rm net}S$ and

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{\rm net}S-1}{S-1},\qquad
\kappa_{\rm net}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\langle v_{k}\rangle
+\rho}{\langle v_{k}\rangle},
\end{equation*}
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{\rm net}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the LTD sign
$\rho<0$ this is $\kappa_{\rm net}=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu<1$ and, up to the
trivial $+1$ self term redefinition, $\kappa_{\rm net}-1=-\rho/\phi\mu$,
the conventionally quoted $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ homeostatic ratio.  Keeping
the filter overlap $I=\int h_{u}h_{v}ds=(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$
explicit the combination which appears is
$\kappa_{\rm dim}=-( \phi\mu+\rho)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})/\phi$, which
reduces to $\kappa_{\rm net}$ after the usual absorption of $I$ in
$\phi$.  This is the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ control parameter quoted in the
literature, i.e. $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ with the sign
convention $\rho\to-\rho$ $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

