offset measured in units of the mean pre-synaptic drive
$\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$.  For the physiological LTD offset
$\rho<0$ the quantity $-\rho/(\phi\mu)$ is $>0$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa$
for $S\to\infty$.  With the conventional shift $c_{kl}\to1+c_{kl}$,
$c_{kk}=0$, the self contribution $1$ is absorbed in $S$ and the
control parameter is quoted in the $1+$ form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa_{0}<1$ for $\rho<0$ and $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for $\rho\to0$.
Up to the inessential $1+$ redefinition and the sign convention
$\rho\to-\rho$ this is the normalised net drive
$(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, often written simply as 
$\kappa\approx |\rho|/(\phi\mu)$ with the sign convention
$\kappa=- \rho/(\phi\mu$).  The $1+$ form is the
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ which is normally quoted as
$c^{*}= \big(\kappa_{0}S-1\big)/(S-1)$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
large spatial summation.  In the bare $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation this is
$\kappa=-\rho/(\phi\mu)$, i.e. $|\kappa|=|\rho|/(\phi\mu)$, the
LTP/LTD balance which sets the large-$S$ asymptotic critical
correlation. ∎

public_text.3:
In the uniform mean field put $w_{k}\equiv w,\;\mu _{k}\equiv \mu $ and
with the fast filter normalisation

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle\tau_{w}
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho \big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle =\phi w\mu^{2}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad 
\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle =w\mu S_{k}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $c_{kk}=1$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1),\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
so that, up to the inessential $w$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the marginal branch

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S \;,
\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{-\dfrac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S-1}{S-1}\; .
\end{equation*}
Writing

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic ratio which controls the threshold is
the offset normalised by the mean pre-synaptic drive

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\qquad{\rm for\;}\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
$|\kappa_{0}|=|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the usual LTD balance $|\rho|=\phi\mu$
for an isolated synapse, $S\to1$.  With the self term $s_{kk}=1$ kept
explicit this is often written in the net-drive form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm net}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
     =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the convention $c_{kl}\to 1+c_{kl}$ used in the text
$1+c(S-1)=\kappa_{\rm net}S$ and

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{\rm net}S-1}{S-1},\qquad
\kappa_{\rm net}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\langle v_{k}\rangle
+\rho}{\langle v_{k}\rangle},
\end{equation*}
$c^{*}\to\kappa_{\rm net}$ for $S\to\infty$.  With the LTD sign
$\rho<0$ this is $\kappa_{\rm net}=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu<1$ and, up to the
trivial $+1$ self term redefinition, $\kappa_{\rm net}-1=-\rho/\phi\mu$,
the conventionally quoted $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ homeostatic ratio.  Keeping
the filter overlap $I=\int h_{u}h_{v}ds=(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$
explicit the combination which appears is
$\kappa_{\rm dim}=-( \phi\mu+\rho)(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})/\phi$, which
reduces to $\kappa_{\rm net}$ after the usual absorption of $I$ in
$\phi$.  This is the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ control parameter quoted in the
literature, i.e. $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu>0$ with the sign
convention $\rho\to-\rho$ $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

4.text:
In the $w$ time scale the two linear traces can be taken quasi static

$\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu_{k}$, $\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}$,
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}$, $c_{kk}=1$,

so that on the mean field

$0=\big\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle $

with

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle
    =\phi w_{k}\mu_{k}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w_{k}\mu_{k}S_{k},\qquad S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\; .
\end{equation*}

Hence up to the inessential $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}

For the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$, $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,
$s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1),
\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1 .
\end{equation*}

Thus

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 ,
\end{equation*}

and on the marginal branch

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S,\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{-\rho S/\phi\mu-1}{S-1} .
\end{equation*}

Writing

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}

the dimensionless homeostatic ratio which sets the threshold is the
additive LTD offset normalised by the mean pre-synaptic drive
$\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\qquad{\rm for\;the\;physiological\;
LTD\;offset\;\rho<0},
\end{equation*}

i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|\kappa|=\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu}=\frac{|\rho|}{\langle v_{k}\rangle}\;,
\end{equation*}

so that $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$ and the isolated synapse
balance $S\to1$ is $-\rho=\phi\mu$, $\kappa=1$, the usual LTP/LTD
balance.

With the conventional $1+$ correlation normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the self term
$s_{kk}=1$ is absorbed in $S$ and the same boundary is quoted in the
net-drive form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\langle v_{k}\rangle+\rho}{\langle v_{k}\rangle}<1
        \;\;{\rm for\;\rho<0},
\end{equation*}

$c^{*}=( \kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$, $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$,
and $\kappa_{0}-1=-\rho/\phi\mu$ is the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ ratio above.
The filter overlap $I=\int h_{u}h_{v}\propto(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$
is absorbed in the normalisation of $\phi$; kept explicit it only
renormalises the overall dimensional prefactor and does not generate a
surviving $\tau_{u}\tau_{v}$ or an explicit $\mu$ inside the
dimensionless $\kappa$.  With the sign convention $\rho\to-\rho$ this is
the familiar positive homeostatic ratio $\kappa\simeq|\rho|/\phi\mu$. ∎

