\end{equation*}
$\kappa_{0}<1$ for the physiological $\rho<0$, $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for
$\rho\to0$, and $\kappa_{0}-1=-\rho/\phi\mu=\kappa$.  With the sign
convention $\rho\to-\rho$ this is the familiar positive control
parameter $\kappa_{0}\simeq |\rho|/\phi\mu$.  The filter overlap
$I=\int h_{u}h_{v}\propto(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$ can be absorbed in the
definition of $\phi$ in the quasi-static normalisation used here, so
that up to the trivial $1+$ self-term shift the homeostatic ratio is the
$|\rho|/\phi\mu$ signal-to-noise balance quoted in the literature.
This is $\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$ in the $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation used
above, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ in the $1+$ convention. ∎

public_text.4:
In the $w$ time scale

${\rm E}\big[\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\big]=0\Longleftrightarrow
{\rm E}[u_{k}v_{k}]+\rho\,{\rm E}[u_{k}]=0 $

and on the $v,u$ time scales
${\rm E}[v_{k}]=\phi\mu_{k}$ , 
${\rm E}[u_{k}]=\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}$ ,
${\rm E}[x_{k}x_{l}]=\mu^{2}c_{kl}$ , $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}[u_{k}v_{k}]
    =\phi w_{k}\mu_{k}^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}\;,
\qquad 
{\rm E}[u_{k}]=w_{k}\mu_{k}S_{k},\qquad 
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the inessential factor $w_{k}\mu_{k}$

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu_{k}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho S_{k}\; .
\end{equation*}

For the homogeneous state $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$,
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\big(S-1\big),\qquad 
S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
On the marginal branch

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S ,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{-\rho S/\phi\mu-1}{S-1}\; .
\end{equation*}
Hence on the percolation form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic control is the LTD offset normalised
by the mean pre-synaptic drive ${\rm E}[v_{k}]=\phi\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\qquad{\rm for\;the\;physiological}
\;\rho<0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|\kappa|=\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu}=\frac{|\rho|}{{\rm E}[v_{k}]},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$ and $c^{*}\to1$ for an isolated
synapse, $-\rho=\phi\mu$, $\kappa=1$, the usual LTP/LTD balance.
The overlap $I=\int h_{u}h_{v}\propto(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$ can be
absorbed in the definition of $\phi$ in the quasi-static normalisation
used here.

With the conventional $1+$ correlation normalisation
${\rm E}[x_{k}x_{l}]=\mu^{2}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=0$, the self term
$s_{kk}=1$ is absorbed in $S$ and the same boundary is normally quoted
in the net-drive form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{{\rm E}[v_{k}]+\rho}{{\rm E}[v_{k}]},
\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
$\kappa_{0}<1$ for $\rho<0$, $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for $\rho\to0$, and
$\kappa_{0}-1=-\rho/\phi\mu=\kappa$.  With the sign convention
$\rho\to-\rho$ this is the familiar positive control parameter
$\kappa_{0}\simeq|\rho|/\phi\mu$, the $|\rho|/\phi\mu$ signal-to-noise
balance.  Up to the trivial $1+$ self-term shift this is the
$\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$ quoted above. ∎

public_text.8:
For the $w$ time scale $\langle \tau_{w}\dot w_{k}\rangle =0$ is

\begin{equation*}
0=\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
and on the $v,u$ time scales in the quasi static limit

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi \mu_{k},\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l},\qquad
\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}c_{kl},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
    =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl},\qquad
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},\qquad
S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}.
\end{equation*}
Up to the inessential factor $w\mu$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}+\rho S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}
In the homogeneous mean field $w_{l}=w,\;\mu_{l}=\mu$,
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$, $s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=s_{kk}+c\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
      =1+c\,(S-1),\qquad S\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}>1,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the marginal branch

\begin{equation*}
1+c^{*}(S-1)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}S,\qquad
c^{*}= \frac{-\rho S/\phi\mu-1}{S-1}\; .
\end{equation*}
Writing the marginal line in the percolation form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless number which controls the threshold is the LTD
offset normalised by the mean pre-synaptic drive
$\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0\qquad\rho<0,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in magnitude

\begin{equation*}
|\kappa|=\frac{|\rho|}{\phi\mu}
       =\frac{|\rho|}{\langle v_{k}\rangle},
\end{equation*}
the additive LTD offset measured in units of the mean pre-synaptic
drive.  In particular $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$ and the isolated
synapse balance $S\to1$ is $-\rho=\phi\mu$, $\kappa=1$.

With the conventional $1+$ correlation normalisation
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\!\left[1+c_{kl}\right]$, $c_{kk}=0$,
the self term $s_{kk}=1$ is absorbed in $S$ and the same boundary is
normally quoted in the net-drive form

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
       =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
       =\frac{\langle v_{k}\rangle+\rho}{\langle v_{k}\rangle},
\qquad c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
$\kappa_{0}<1$ for the physiological $\rho<0$, $\kappa_{0}\to1$ for
$\rho\to0$, and $\kappa_{0}-1=-\rho/\phi\mu=\kappa$.  With the sign
convention $\rho\to-\rho$ this is the familiar positive control
parameter $\kappa_{0}\simeq |\rho|/\phi\mu$.  The filter overlap
$I=\int h_{u}h_{v}\propto(\tau_{u}+\tau_{v})^{-1}$ can be absorbed in the
definition of $\phi$ in the quasi-static normalisation used here, so
that up to the trivial $1+$ self-term shift the homeostatic ratio is the
$|\rho|/\phi\mu$ signal-to-noise balance quoted in the literature.
This is $\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu$ in the $c_{kk}=1$ normalisation used
above, i.e. $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ in the $1+$ convention. ∎
